WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.757
The query here is long, so do not try to read every symbol from beginning to end.

00:00:05.757 --> 00:00:09.782
Instead, read it as a sequence of stages.

00:00:09.782 --> 00:00:14.072
The first common table expression is called normalized.

00:00:14.072 --> 00:00:19.712
It cleans the basic text representations while keeping the raw date string.

00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:22.287
The next stage is called typed.

00:00:22.287 --> 00:00:27.652
It attempts to convert only supported date formats into real date values.

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:30.307
The third stage is called ranked.

00:00:30.307 --> 00:00:36.033
It calculates duplicate ranking using normalized identity fields and a completeness rule.

00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:42.620
Finally, the outer query returns cleaned analytical fields and assigns a row status.

00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:49.805
This is one reason common table expressions are useful: they let us break a complex cleaning pipeline into

00:00:49.805 --> 00:00:53.545
understandable steps. Run the whole query once.

00:00:53.545 --> 00:00:57.274
Then use the next few slides to study each stage separately.
